  {
    "label": "72c45146-95bd-53af-b2f0-49b5ef23b12e",
    "record": {
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "72c45146-95bd-53af-b2f0-49b5ef23b12e",
        ":question/problem": "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061xy$ and $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=\u2207f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{\u2032}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\u2207f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207f(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061xy)\\,i+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061xy)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{xy}\\,i+\\frac{x}{xy}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,i+\\frac{1}{y}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t$ and $y={t}^{2}$ into the above to find $\u2207f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{\u2032}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\,i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =\u2207f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{\u2032}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\,i+2t\\,j) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t}+\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t}-\\operatorname{tan}\u2061t \\\\  & =\\frac{2-t\\operatorname{tan}\u2061t}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
