    "label": "240fc49a-d022-5d2c-b6ad-58058500b98b",
    "record": {
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "240fc49a-d022-5d2c-b6ad-58058500b98b",
        ":question/problem": "If $f(x,y)={x}^{4}-{y}^{4}$ and $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=\u2207f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{\u2032}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\u2207f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207f(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}({x}^{4}-{y}^{4})\\,i+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}({x}^{4}-{y}^{4})\\,j \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}\\,i-4{y}^{3}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t$ into the above to find $\u2207f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207f(r(t)) & =4{cos}^{3}\u2061t\\,i-4{sin}^{3}\u2061t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{\u2032}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =\u2207f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{\u2032}(t) \\\\  & =(4{cos}^{3}\u2061t\\,i-4{sin}^{3}\u2061t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t\\,j) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t{cos}^{3}\u2061t-4{sin}^{3}\u2061t\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t({cos}^{2}\u2061t+{sin}^{2}\u2061t) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
