  {
    "label": "2ad8b1a6-b8e1-5739-bfbc-4544e317c6e3",
    "record": {
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "2ad8b1a6-b8e1-5739-bfbc-4544e317c6e3",
        ":question/problem": "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{3}y)$ and $r(t)=t\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=\u2207f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{\u2032}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\u2207f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207f(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{3}y)\\,i+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{3}y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}y}\\,i+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{x}\\,i+\\frac{1}{y}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t$ and $y={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $\u2207f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207f(r(t)) & =\\frac{3}{t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{\u2032}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =\u2207f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{\u2032}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}}\\,j)\\cdot (\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{t}+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{t}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{t+3}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
            ":answer-field/id": "306c4e3a-7620-56d0-8ab5-267aba392210",
