        ":question/id": "b5969d89-d7a6-55d3-97d8-1ebc95c98dd0",
        ":question/problem": "If $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+xy$ and $r(t)=(t+1)\\,i-{t}^{2}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=\u2207f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{\u2032}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\u2207f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207f(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,i+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x+y)\\,i+x\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t+1$ and $y=-{t}^{2}$ into the above to find $\u2207f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207f(r(t)) & =(2t+2-{t}^{2})\\,i+(t+1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{\u2032}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =\\,i-2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =\u2207f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{\u2032}(t) \\\\  & =((2t+2-{t}^{2})\\,i+(t+1)\\,j)\\cdot (\\,i-2t\\,j) \\\\  & =2t+2-{t}^{2}-2t(t+1) \\\\  & =2t+2-{t}^{2}-2{t}^{2}-2t \\\\  & =2-3{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
